# The Conversation That Could Have Happened

*The following is a counterfactual reconstruction. It is not presented as a conversation that occurred or as evidence of the words of either participant. Its purpose is to identify the clinical and human work that remained available in April 2018.*

The neurologist might have begun by returning to the last difficult moment between them.

“Liam, before you left hospital, you told me that we had given you the full NCT and sent you out on bald tyres. I understood what you meant. We had investigated you extensively, but you felt that we had not properly attended to your feet—the part of the problem that was causing you pain and affecting every step you took. I want to acknowledge that directly before we discuss anything else.”

Liam might have answered:

“That was the point. I was not denying what you had found. I understood that there was something seriously wrong neurologically. But I came into care because of my feet and my walking. I could not understand how I could leave with a major neurological diagnosis while the thing that brought me there was still being sent back towards Podiatry.”

The neurologist could then have restored the uncertainty honestly:

“The discharge record said that the relationship was ‘Not clear.’ Looking back, that wording did not mean we had established that the feet were separate. It meant that we had not resolved the relationship. We saw corns, calluses, fallen arches and other structural features, but we also saw a gait that looked somewhat dystonic. Those findings could coexist. Sending you to Podiatry might address pressure and structure, but it did not answer the neurological question.”

He might then have addressed the Procyclidine evidence:

“You have now told us something important. When Procyclidine was withdrawn, the bodily tension returned, your feet became restless, the abnormal foot problem reappeared and your walking deteriorated. That does not, by itself, prove one diagnosis or establish that every element of the pain is neurological. But it demonstrates that the state of your feet and gait is neurologically modifiable. That evidence must be allowed to affect how we understand the original problem.”

Liam might have replied:

“That is why I did not keep repeating the argument from the hospital. I thought you knew now. I thought the medication response had made the point better than I ever could. I was not expecting you to agree with every explanation I had. I expected the feet to remain inside the neurological investigation.”

The neurologist could have answered:

“That was a reasonable expectation. We were extremely cautious about levodopa because of your age, the prospect of many years of treatment and concern about long-term motor complications. Professor Counihan and our colleagues advised great care. But caution is not the same as refusing to obtain information. A treatment strategy must remain provisional when material evidence changes.”

He might then have described the available medical choice:

“We should now decide whether a carefully controlled, low-dose dopaminergic trial would provide useful information about your gait, rigidity, bodily tension and feet. That would not mean committing you immediately to unrestricted long-term levodopa. It would mean defining the symptoms beforehand, observing you in a controlled state, administering a cautious dose if clinically appropriate and recording what changes. If we consider that unsafe or uninformative, we should document why and obtain movement-disorders advice.”

Liam might have said:

“I could accept caution if I knew the question was still open. What I could not accept was living with the pain while everyone behaved as though Podiatry had answered it.”

The neurologist might then have made the responsibility explicit:

“Podiatry does not own the entire question. Neurology will retain responsibility for determining whether dystonia, rigidity, abnormal loading, an OFF state or another neurological mechanism contributes to the feet. We will record that you challenged the earlier disposition, that the relationship remains unresolved and that the Procyclidine response requires reassessment. Future clinicians should not mistake your understanding of the Parkinsonian diagnosis for agreement that the feet were separate.”

That conversation would not have required an admission of negligence. It would not have required the neurologist to accept that Liam’s entire theory was correct. It would have required only candour about what remained unknown, recognition that the patient’s objection had been clinically intelligent and permission for subsequent evidence to alter the foundation.

As far as the disclosed record demonstrates, that restorative conversation never occurred. The Protest was not returned to the longitudinal history. The Procyclidine response generated management activity but did not visibly reconstruct the original foot disposition. The patient was not told that the evidence he regarded as resolving the dispute had been accepted prospectively without being permitted its full retrospective significance.

The governing force was the senior-backed policy of exceptional dopaminergic restraint. It would be unsafe to describe that policy as an absolute prohibition or to claim that Professor Counihan personally ordered that levodopa could never be used. The documentary evidence nevertheless shows that restraint carried extraordinary authority: ordinary dopaminergic treatment was to be left off for as long as possible, and another specialist was effectively invited to displace a position already held by “myself and Tim.”

The Protest did not create that management plan. It confronted the plan with a choice. The treating team could preserve the plan as provisional while delaying discharge, examining the feet more closely, seeking movement-disorders input or considering a small supervised diagnostic trial. Alternatively, it could proceed with discharge and allow the existing restraint policy and Podiatry disposition to govern. The omission of the Protest concealed the fact that this choice had arisen.

A cautious levodopa challenge was not a trivial or risk-free “procedure,” and expert evidence would be required before alleging that competent practice demanded it. But it was an identifiable clinical option. So were further examination, senior reconsideration and a documented decision explaining why a trial should not occur. The important failure is not yet proof that levodopa had to be given. It is the absence of a visible patient-specific adjudication of whether neurological state-responsiveness should be tested when the disabling feet remained “Not clear,” appeared potentially dystonic and had become the subject of an express protest against discharge.

The profound human loss lies in how little reconciliation would have required. The neurologist did not have to surrender his clinical judgment. Liam did not have to prove his diagnosis. Someone needed only to say: “I remember what you told me. The feet remain part of the neurological question. The plan is not allowed to erase that.”

Instead, the management plan survived, the Protest disappeared from the authoritative account, and the patient carried forward the belief that Neurology knew what he had shown them. Years later, the institution relied upon the continuity produced inside that account as evidence that the course had always been coherent.

That is why the missing conversation matters. It represents the point at which clinical authority could have become accountable without becoming defensive, uncertainty could have remained productive rather than terminal, and the patient’s objection could have been converted into a reassessment pathway instead of being left outside the history that governed his future.

